00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.580
why did we launch this.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.220
So a couple of things
that have been happening.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.770
Unless you've been living
under a rock, you know this.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.710
Organizations are changing,
because their users are

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.140
changing, the user
expectations are changing.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.030
As people are bringing
in mobile devices

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.030
into work, as they're
getting familiar and loving

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.030
cloud-based services, they're
expecting the same experience

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:03.870
at work.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.980
And that's putting new pressure
on IT and organizations,

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.960
in general, to make
those users happy

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.320
and give them a way of
work which matches how they

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.780
work in the consumer world.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.530
And this is not going
to go down anytime soon.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.630
If anything, organizations
are increasingly

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.140
telling us that they are going
to go all in on the cloud.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.840
The amount of cloud services,
the number of cloud services

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.540
that they're going to be
using is only going up.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.000
And three or four
years ago, people

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.940
were worried about laws and
compliance and governance.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.040
And there was genuinely
a question of,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.050
is cloud more of a hype and
will cloud really happen?

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.850
Now, that's no longer the case.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.500
Customers are clearly
saying that they

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.031
are going to move to the cloud.

00:02:48.031 --> 00:02:50.280
The question is when and how
do they actually do that.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.860
So cloud is very real.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.640
And then we have data
that shows that--

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.670
this is from Gartner--

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.792
which says that, by 2021,
more than 50% of organizations

00:02:57.792 --> 00:02:59.250
will have an all-in
cloud strategy.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.650
So really, an easier
way of saying this

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.690
is more cloud and majority
cloud is happening.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.640
So you guys, users saying,
give me more cloud,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.660
and organizations
are saying, yes, we

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.052
are ready to embrace more cloud.

00:03:13.052 --> 00:03:14.760
And then there are
other things happening

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.176
from an infrastructure
standpoint.

00:03:16.176 --> 00:03:18.160
Let me just outline
that for you.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.710
We've heard about
containers today.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.440
And generally, what it takes
to build a cloud service today

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.040
is just getting
increasingly simple.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.800
People are becoming really
comfortable with microservices.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.950
Containers are taking off,
and Kubernetes, in particular,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.780
is very, very popular.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:42.870
We have some data that shows 40%
growth with just the Kubernetes

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.380
deployment in an organization.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.350
And if you look at
what Urs shared,

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.060
earlier today, that
number is even higher

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.920
if you think about
just awareness

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.750
and willing to adopt Kubernetes.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.560
So all this is saying that
users want more cloud,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.480
organizations are ready
to move to the cloud.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.160
Application
architectures are making

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.480
it easy to move to the cloud.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.370
So with all of this
happening, there's

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.350
a new thing that's
happening that organizations

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:05.790
are struggling with.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.100
Organizations,
historically, last 20 years,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.290
have been in this
world where they

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.700
are on this corporate network.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.990
They still assume trust.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.420
They believe that being
on the corporate network

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.829
is how you get secure.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:17.769
That made sense.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:19.988
It made sense, because
your applications

00:04:19.988 --> 00:04:21.029
were behind the firewall.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.210
Your data was
behind the firewall.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.400
So it made sense
to protect that.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.260
And that's how organizations
and their architectures

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.002
were set up.

00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:30.960
Fundamentally, what's
happening with the cloud,

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.150
as you all know, the
applications are in the cloud.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.770
More importantly,
the data that is

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.750
associated with
those applications

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.410
are also now in the cloud.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.185
So what are you protecting
that's behind the perimeter?

00:04:41.185 --> 00:04:42.060
There's not too much.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.509
And if this trend continues,
everything is in the cloud.

00:04:44.509 --> 00:04:46.050
So the whole idea,
that you're trying

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.590
to protect something, which is
a corporate network making trust

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.130
decisions and access decisions
based on this access perimeter,

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.600
is no longer valid.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.400
And applications
are in the cloud.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.430
And then, besides the data,
who are you collaborating with?

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.395
It's an increasingly
collaborative world,

00:05:01.395 --> 00:05:03.260
an increasingly
interconnected world.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.890
So you're not just working with
your fellow employees that may

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.942
be distributed at other sites.

00:05:07.942 --> 00:05:09.150
You're working with partners.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.620
You're working with
your customers.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.203
And you're working
with other vendors,

00:05:12.203 --> 00:05:14.370
who are not going to get
on your corporate network.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.710
So this whole idea, of
being on a corporate network

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.580
and that meaning anything,
is completely moot

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.870
as organizations
move to the cloud.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.500
So last but not
least, we now have

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.690
IT working on a
bolt-on strategy, which

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.170
is, hey, the old way of
connecting to the secure thing

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.180
was using something like a VPN.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.650
That may or may not always work.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.550
It was complex.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.310
If you forgot your RSA
secure ID somewhere,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.810
you just couldn't
get your work done

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.420
till you got back to a spot
that actually had that.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.490
So these are problematic.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.280
And you cannot have an
architecture and an access

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.350
model that is a bolt-on.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.100
You cannot have cloud access
models built around how you

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.210
architected and delivered
solutions to your end users

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.034
in a predominantly
on-premises-based world.

00:05:59.034 --> 00:06:00.450
So that is the key
insight for us,

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:05.310
where we started to think about,
if the perimeter is no longer

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.810
the corporate network and users
are looking for an access model

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.000
that's easy, simple,
consumer-like,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.750
then how do we build a
solution, from the ground up,

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:17.000
that gives you really
the holy grail--

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.010
the idea that you
can deliver usability

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.140
that is consumer-like,
at the same time,

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.630
you can give advanced security
that gives peace of mind

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:24.860
to organizations?

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.380
So for that, we built a new
solution, from the ground up,

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:28.640
architected for the cloud.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:31.070
You really cannot take an
on-prem solution and bolt this

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:31.580
on.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:33.204
So, in fact, we were
privileged that we

00:06:33.204 --> 00:06:36.600
were able to build a cloud-based
solution from the ground up.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.540
There were three
key teams that lead

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:39.920
to how we built our solution.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.260
And we'll get into
it a little bit.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.930
But the first thing is
really about security.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:48.800
We recognize that we can talk
about innovation all day,

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.840
but, if you don't have
a secure foundation,

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.706
no organization is
going to touch it.

00:06:52.706 --> 00:06:54.830
So we have to make sure
this thing is fundamentally

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:56.430
secure from the ground up.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:57.920
And that's a key theme for us.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.160
Second, there is so much going
on in the cloud that you cannot

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:05.900
rely on an access model, you
cannot rely on static reactive

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:06.440
policies.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.890
We really believe that the
world has to be about smarts,

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.930
where we can combine
admin intent--

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:13.790
the admin always has
to be in control--

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:16.520
but we need to proactively
protect the users with data

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:19.560
that companies like
Google can provide.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.690
So a quick example, right?

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.940
Google is really good
at crawling the web.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.890
And we know what the
good websites are.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.800
And that's what we give you
when you do a Search result

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.080
or when you run a Search query.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.940
But we're also equally
good at crawling

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.160
what we know are
fishy, bad websites,

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.680
and we make sure that the
users never get to those.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.650
That's not a reactive
policy that you need to set.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.390
That's something that we can
proactively protect for you.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.510
In fact, later on, in
our context of our demos

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.700
that Vidya will have,
we'll give you a glimmer

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.590
of what this could be.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:51.260
And this, it'll be something
that you'll see us rollout,

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.690
increasingly, in
the quarters ahead.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.670
Last but not the least,
we had to make sure

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.170
that this thing
is really simple.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:58.720
Sorry.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.386
This thing is really simple.

00:08:00.386 --> 00:08:01.760
People have told
us that, as they

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.010
put these solutions
together, they really

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:05.300
struggle with the complexity.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.550
They deal with multiple
vendors coming in.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:09.790
There's white papers that
they go to put together.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:12.110
And there's never really
that piece of mind

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.304
that tells you that I
got my solution right.

00:08:14.304 --> 00:08:16.595
They worry if they've paid
too much for their software.

00:08:16.595 --> 00:08:18.926
They worry if they got too
much software going on.

00:08:18.926 --> 00:08:20.300
And more importantly,
they wonder

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.070
if they actually got it right.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:25.400
So one of the things that we
really focus on is simplicity.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.120
And simplicity is in the end
user experience, simplicity

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:30.320
in the admin experience.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.120
So we give you a
single unified UI.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:35.159
Sorry, there's a timer built in.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:38.450
A single unified UI that means
you can manage everything,

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:41.510
from people, applications, and
devices, all from one console.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:44.480
And last but not the least, we
have a very simple SKU model.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.010
We have a free SKU.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:48.830
And we have one
premium or paid SKU.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.320
You get one license,
and that's one price.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.240
And you never have to
worry about that price,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.365
ever again, as we deliver
additional functionality.

00:08:55.365 --> 00:08:57.650
So there's simplicity
all the way through,

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:01.000
from the product experience,
to the product licensing.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.220
So let's dive
into, very quickly,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.370
what is Cloud Identity.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.760
It is what we call a
cloud-based solution,

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.490
a single, unified solution to
manage people, applications,

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:11.420
and devices.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:13.170
So this means not
just your employees,

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.140
but also your
customers and partners.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.750
It also means your
applications, everything

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:19.940
from cloud-based applications
to other applications

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:21.840
you may be using in
your organization.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.080
And finally,
devices-- we, today,

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.332
support iOS and Android as well
as Chrome, but, in the future,

00:09:27.332 --> 00:09:29.790
we will be supporting the other
desktop platforms, as well,

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:31.998
all available through one
unified console and product

00:09:31.998 --> 00:09:33.620
experience.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:35.360
Last but not the least,
this is something

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.450
that we introduced
recently, but it is not new.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:41.750
For years, what we did was we
were bundling Cloud Identity

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:44.630
inside G Suite, and we were
using that to also serve

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.010
GCP customers.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:48.740
What we were really trying to
do was-- is this something that

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.150
is resonating with customers?

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.220
Is this something that
we should find out

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.080
if we should launch
as a standalone entity

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:55.070
in the market?

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:58.160
And the overwhelming
feedback from our customers

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.870
was, yes, please do.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:02.750
We have all kinds of users, who
are not knowledge workers, who

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:06.200
do not need collaboration
software, who may not always

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.870
need all the access to
all the apps on GCP,

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.460
but they absolutely need
user and device management.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.210
And over time, we may be rolling
them on to GCP apps and things

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:15.710
like that.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.309
So that is one of the key
pieces that came for us.

00:10:18.309 --> 00:10:20.475
The feedback was strong
with our existing customers.

00:10:20.475 --> 00:10:22.400
So we really took that out.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:25.074
And we made sure that, from a
pricing, packaging standpoint,

00:10:25.074 --> 00:10:26.990
this was available
standalone, which is really

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:28.160
what we've delivered here.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.510
So it's available
standalone, but it's not new.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:31.580
It's been battle-tested.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.580
In fact, we have thousands
of customers and millions

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.290
of users and devices
under management,

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:40.730
because we've been rolling
this as part of G Suite.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.889
Very quickly, what is
in a Cloud Identity?

00:10:42.889 --> 00:10:45.430
And what is we call the "single
pane of glass," a unified UI?

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:49.070
You'll get all the typical use
cases that you would expect,

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:51.860
from a cloud-based
identity-as-a-service solution,

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.410
as well as a unified endpoint
or enterprise mobility solution.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.455
So specifically, we have a
user lifecycle management,

00:10:58.455 --> 00:11:00.170
so everything from
a user on-boarding,

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:01.930
user off-boarding,
when an employee

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:05.150
gets hired as well as fired,
or changes jobs and things

00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:05.650
like that.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:06.980
Everything is covered there.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.650
People always are worried
about account security.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:11.576
And I'll get into
that in more detail.

00:11:11.576 --> 00:11:12.950
But we can protect
your user, not

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:15.020
just from a username
password perspective,

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.110
but additional multi-factor
auth systems that you need.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:19.440
People want single sign-on.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.001
They want automated provisioning
of their user accounts.

00:11:22.001 --> 00:11:23.750
That's what we call
SaaS app provisioning.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:24.590
We have that.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.300
And we have iOS and
Android management,

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.130
along with all the
other fundamentals,

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:33.610
like an admin console,
reporting APIs, all of that.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:37.530
So what I'll do next is just get
into, specifically, why Google,

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:41.410
and then we'll dive into each of
those big pillar product areas.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.130
So this is key for us.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:45.850
And this is, at
least, how we believe

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:47.430
and how we look at the market.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:50.632
When we started to work
on cloud-based identity

00:11:50.632 --> 00:11:52.090
and cloud-based
identity solutions,

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:53.890
there was always
this classic problem.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:57.510
Can we actually deliver a
user solution that's simple?

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.200
And can we actually
make it secure?

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:01.380
So can we give advanced
access to users

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:05.260
while preserving the advanced
security that admins need?

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.690
And so that was
really key for us.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:10.020
And as part of BeyondCorp-- and
Vidya will walk through that--

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:12.930
that's something we
battle-tested within Google.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.060
I joined Google two years ago.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:19.020
I set my password on my
first day at orientation.

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:23.170
I have never changed my password
in my two years I've been here.

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:26.340
I've never typed my
RSA secure ID key,

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.160
because I don't have one.

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.150
I just use security keys.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.110
And so I just marveled at
the way I used to work.

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:35.310
And I felt like
this is fantastic.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:37.290
And coming from an
enterprise background,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:40.170
that's really what we wanted
to enable for everyone.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:41.440
We did that through security.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.770
We did that by
being really smart.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.220
Like I said earlier, we
think that ML data is really

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.530
going to come in and
protect people, proactively.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.870
The heritage that we have--
we have seven services

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.420
that are a billion-plus,
30-day active count, which

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:58.290
means we have tons of data about
what is good and what is bad.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.060
And we can use that
to set policies.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.690
In fact, that will
be part of the demo.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:03.842
And I already talked
about the simplicity.

00:13:03.842 --> 00:13:05.300
So let's dive into
the key pillars.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:06.960
The first thing is
user management.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.520
Everybody knows that, if
your user is compromised,

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:11.820
then somebody can
impersonate that user

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.070
and get access to
really sensitive data.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:15.560
So people are
looking for, hey, I

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:18.240
need more than password-based
authentication.

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:19.860
They need two-step
authentication.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.530
And I'll talk about security
keys on the next slide.

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.710
But we give you whatever works
for you from a two-factor auth

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:29.099
perspective, as well as
the security key piece

00:13:29.099 --> 00:13:30.390
that I'll just talk about next.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:32.970
But ultimately, we know
that, from a security keys

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.609
and from a user
account standpoint,

00:13:35.609 --> 00:13:37.150
there's tons of data
out there, which

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.650
says that, for example, by
Google's own internal estimate,

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:44.400
we think there are more than 1.9
billion usernames and passwords

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.449
floating around in the dark web.

00:13:46.449 --> 00:13:48.990
This means that that password,
that your employee is setting,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.790
is likely already compromised.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.130
So it doesn't mean that you
can rely on passwords alone

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.240
to protect them.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.590
You need this additional
factor of authentication.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.840
So let's look at what we
did with security keys.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:01.350
But if you like your
one-time-use passcodes

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.240
and you like push
notifications, while we

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:05.152
don't think they
are as secure, we

00:14:05.152 --> 00:14:06.360
want to give you that choice.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.090
Because, ultimately, you
may have a business reason

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:11.270
while you're ruling that out.

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:13.190
So let's talk account security.

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.620
Prabhakar talked about this
a little bit, this morning,

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:16.760
in the keynote.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:20.650
But since we rolled out security
keys for all the Googlers,

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:23.390
we've had zero instances
of their accounts being

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:24.380
compromised.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.740
And Google, because I go back
to our consumer heritage,

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:29.885
we don't actually
use that here, but we

00:14:29.885 --> 00:14:31.970
are able to use the
data in a way that

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:33.380
protects organizations.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:36.600
Because we can now come in.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.810
We are constantly trying
to get attacked and hacked,

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:40.850
which means that we
know and can detect

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:42.320
what an attack looks like.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.770
And we can apply
the same practices,

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:48.530
in proactively supporting
and protecting your users,

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:49.776
for your organization.

00:14:49.776 --> 00:14:50.900
So that's account security.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.624
Let's keep your
users really secure.

00:14:53.624 --> 00:14:55.915
The next thing that we talk
about is device management.

00:14:55.915 --> 00:14:58.227
A lot of times, when
people think identity,

00:14:58.227 --> 00:14:59.810
they think about
user identity, right?

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:03.590
So Karthik is an employee,
with employee ID 1234.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:05.510
That's no longer
super-interesting.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.980
That is important, but
it's not the only thing.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.550
We look at things that
are about me, as a user,

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:13.191
but also think about
additional context

00:15:13.191 --> 00:15:14.190
that might be happening.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.010
So, as an example, who am I?

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:18.500
What kind of role do I
have in this organization?

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:20.360
What kind of
privileges do I have?

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:24.510
What kind of devices am I using
to access these applications?

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.660
All of that goes
into the attributes

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.030
of making a really smart
decision about, should I

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:32.402
get access, how long should I
get access, things like that.

00:15:32.402 --> 00:15:34.610
All of that happens if we
can bring device management

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:35.630
into the fold.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:37.790
So we offer the same
EMM capabilities,

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:41.420
to manage iOS and Android,
that you might be using

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:42.920
in your organization already.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:44.540
And very soon, as
a road map feature,

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:47.545
we will have Windows and
Mac management, as well.

00:15:47.545 --> 00:15:49.170
So that's device
management, because we

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:50.730
look at the device
signals, as well,

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:54.930
to additionally protect the
users and protect the accounts.

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:57.540
So one of the things that are
probably not very well known--

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:01.020
and this goes back to us
bundling Cloud Identity as part

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:01.950
of G Suite--

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:05.280
is that we've been actively
used by organizations.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:09.300
And Gartner, in
particular, recognized us.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:10.800
And this is not a Gartner vote.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.170
This is customers coming out
and voting for our identity

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.110
solution, our device management
solution as a customer

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:17.730
favorite.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.410
So you already get
a particular rating.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:23.820
And once you go above that,
that's when you get the badge.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:28.520
And this is something
we just earned in 2018.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.650
So it's the Customer Choice
for our MDM solution.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:32.480
We have more than
10 million devices

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:35.720
that are under 30-day
active management.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.730
So let's talk about the
application management.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:40.280
We have a rich catalog
of applications.

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:41.772
People need single sign-on.

00:16:41.772 --> 00:16:44.230
They don't want to come in and
configure those applications

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:46.319
for single sign-on, and
they're error-prone.

00:16:46.319 --> 00:16:48.110
So we just give you a
preconfigured catalog

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:49.100
out of the box.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.260
We support SAML and OIDC today.

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:53.690
And we will soon be launching
password vaulting, which

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.235
is your password-based SSO.

00:16:55.235 --> 00:16:57.110
So if you have a
nonstandard application that

00:16:57.110 --> 00:16:59.897
doesn't support the
existing standards,

00:16:59.897 --> 00:17:01.730
and they just use
passwords, we can give you

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:02.813
a single sign-on for that.

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:07.250
One last thing on
application management.

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.359
We have a lot of apps
under management.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:10.770
Another quick show of hands.

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:14.540
How many of you have heard about
Cloud Identity-Aware Proxy?

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:18.400
Not as many people in the room.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:20.260
But this is a great
way for organizations

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.645
to modernize their applications
or even lift and shift

00:17:22.645 --> 00:17:23.829
their applications.

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:28.089
If you run them on GCP, and
then you have an Identity-Aware

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:30.970
Proxy, what it does is it
looks at your identity-- again,

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:34.030
user state, your device state,
and additional context--

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.400
and it can make decisions
about your applications.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:39.650
We call these BeyondCorp for
your second-party applications.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.160
So the idea, that you can
take your modern applications,

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:45.220
lift and shift applications,
run them on GCP,

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:48.250
and then give them the
same protection as any

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:49.750
of the other
applications, happens

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:51.384
through Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:17:51.384 --> 00:17:53.800
So when you sign up for Cloud
Identity, this is an option.

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:55.300
You can always
use that if you're

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.100
looking at modernizing
your applications.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:00.850
With that, let me
introduce Daniel.

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:03.300
He's an amazing customer for us.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.680
He has a foundationally
amazing story

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:10.150
of how he used a small group
of employees, on his team,

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:13.180
to manage a very large
employee organization

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:14.810
purely using Cloud Identity.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.360
So let me invite Daniel.

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:17.644
And he will tell you more.

00:18:17.644 --> 00:18:18.310
Welcome, Daniel.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:18.840
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:19.923
DANIEL TEHRANI: Thank you.

00:18:19.923 --> 00:18:24.060
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.337
Thanks.

00:18:26.337 --> 00:18:27.420
My name is Daniel Tehrani.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:29.790
I am vice president of
IT at HealthChannels.

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:31.630
I've been with the
company about six years.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:32.880
And in that time,
I've guided them

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.800
through implementing
multiple cloud products,

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.410
the first of which was G Suite.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.810
But before I get started,
I want to talk a little bit

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:41.590
about our company.

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:44.070
HealthChannels was
established 14 years ago,

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.320
as Scribe America,
and has since grown

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:49.170
to incorporate three
distinct organizations that

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.810
provide services to
over 2,600 locations

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:56.554
and employ 17,000
employees, internationally.

00:18:56.554 --> 00:18:58.220
Our primary line of
business is scribes.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.620
And a scribe is somebody that
follows the physician around

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.177
and assists them with their
clinical documentation.

00:19:07.415 --> 00:19:08.790
Some of the biggest
challenges we

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:11.130
faced within our
organization, when

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.470
enabling our digital
workplace, was we're

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:14.610
a high-growth organization.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:18.690
To give you an idea, in 2014,
we had approximately 5,000

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:19.710
employees.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.014
Today, we have 17,000.

00:19:22.014 --> 00:19:24.180
We also experienced a
significant amount of turnover

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:25.980
within our organization.

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.740
We hire, predominantly,
pre-med students

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:31.140
that are looking to move
on into medical careers.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.915
They last with us
about two years.

00:19:32.915 --> 00:19:36.660
But we also have a
stringent training process

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.610
that not everybody gets through.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:41.560
All of that amounts to a
turnover of about 1,000 people

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:42.060
every month.

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:50.090
Lastly, we run a
very small IT group.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:54.290
Total headcount is
about six people.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:57.820
And we do that, and we have
to administer multiple--

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:00.440
an ever-growing list
of applications as we

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:03.450
adopted more and more
cloud applications.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.200
So how did we deal
with all of that?

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:08.990
So we dealt with
that by leveraging

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:12.440
Workday's Reports-as-a-Service
and Google Cloud

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:15.920
Identity's APIs to automate our
on-boarding and off-boarding

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:19.350
processes, thus eliminating
the need for IT to get involve

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:21.000
to provision accounts.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.890
We also leveraged Google's
automatic provisioning,

00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:30.420
into third party applications,
and deprovisioning, as well,

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:32.450
which also removed
the need for us

00:20:32.450 --> 00:20:35.220
to provision our users
into those applications,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:39.140
giving them the tools
they need from day one.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.330
Lastly, we've simplified
our login process,

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:43.430
by enabling all of
our applications,

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:47.870
through the G Suite SSO,
and enabled multi-factor

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:49.700
within our
organizations, securing

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:52.580
not only G Suite
and our applications

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:54.880
but our third party
applications requiring

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:58.470
MFA to get into those, as well.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:03.200
So why did we pick the Google
stack or Google Cloud Identity?

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:05.180
Number one was the
familiarity of the product.

00:21:07.707 --> 00:21:09.665
Number one was the
familiarity of the product--

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:15.460
If it stays, it stays.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.080
If it doesn't, oh, well--

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:18.880
was the familiarity
of the product

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.960
to our end users
and our employees.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.880
They are used to using G
Suite from higher education

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:28.550
and in their personal
lives, as well.

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:30.880
The other part of it was
familiarity of our admins,

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:31.570
right?

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:32.530
They use G Suite.

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:35.030
They've been using G Suite
for the past six years.

00:21:35.030 --> 00:21:38.228
So leveraging Cloud Identity
was a natural progression.

00:21:41.096 --> 00:21:41.870
It's OK.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:50.015
Additionally, we also use--

00:21:50.015 --> 00:21:56.280
all right-- Additionally,
Google's focus

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:58.260
on simplicity and
security really

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:00.660
made it easy for us
to adopt multi-factor

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:03.150
within our organization.

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:07.200
So by that, Google
provides push notifications

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:10.350
to people's Android
or through Gmail,

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.040
as well as security
keys, which makes

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:14.940
it really easy to get through
that multi-factor portion

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:16.484
of it.

00:22:16.484 --> 00:22:18.900
Additionally, there's simplicity
in security from an admin

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:20.790
perspective, as well.

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:24.780
When a user is on-boarded
into Cloud Identity,

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:26.820
you can set a period
of time in which they

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:31.250
have to set up multi-factor.

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:33.720
And once they log
in, they are prompted

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:37.220
to go through the multi-factor
setup, and they're set.

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:39.420
They're ready to go
with multi-factor.

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:42.270
So that decreases
the level of support

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:45.790
that IT needed to provide.

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:49.090
Lastly, Google's extensible
APIs and the reliability

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:51.850
really allowed us to automate
and move things forward

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:53.680
without any hindrances.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:58.170
We did all of that
within a couple of weeks.

00:22:58.170 --> 00:22:59.670
So looking forward,
we're looking

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.670
to further automate
our employee lifecycle

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:05.280
and implement more
smarts, which Vidya

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:07.980
will talk about a
little bit later,

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:10.780
as well as implement device
management and Cloud Identity

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.890
for external consultants.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.190
With all that said, I'd like
to pass the baton to Vidya

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:18.940
to talk about it, anyway.

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:24.837
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:24.837 --> 00:23:26.170
VIDYA NAGARAJAN: Thanks, Daniel.

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:26.870
Hi, everyone.

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:27.500
I'm Vidya.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:30.140
And I'm one of the product
leads on Cloud Identity.

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:32.200
So we have been
working quite hard,

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:36.010
as a team, to solve many of
the access control problems,

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.470
that our customers
are seeing, along

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:41.560
the foundational principles
of being smart, being

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:43.870
secure, and being simple.

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.970
So there are a few
exciting features

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:48.670
that are in the horizon, that
I'd like to now talk about.

00:23:52.642 --> 00:23:53.350
How many of you--

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:58.150
I think, we spoke about it--
know about what BeyondCorp is?

00:23:58.150 --> 00:23:59.470
Great.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:03.400
So BeyondCorp is an
enterprise security model

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:06.100
that Google was a pioneer of.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:10.060
It was envisioned, within
Google, in the year 2011,

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:13.360
because of the motivation
of increasing simplicity,

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:15.880
usability, as well
as security, so

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:18.130
that every employee
at Google can

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:23.080
work from untrusted networks
in a very secure manner

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:26.960
without using and setting
up traditional VPN networks.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:31.330
So with that goal, the
BeyondCorp security model

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:34.360
essentially moves
access controls,

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:38.410
from your network perimeter, to
individual users and devices,

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:40.600
so that every user
can work really

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:44.500
securely from any location.

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:48.930
So in this new world, and with
this new BeyondCorp paradigm,

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:54.090
Cloud Identity helps bring
BeyondCorp to our customers.

00:24:54.090 --> 00:24:58.380
In this world, user,
device, and context

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:01.200
is the new security perimeter.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:03.030
Google is very
uniquely positioned in

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:06.240
that we can protect our
customers, with ML data,

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.150
across over seven services,
with over 1 billion

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:11.380
30-day active users.

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:15.030
So we have the context of
the user and the request,

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:17.100
namely which device
and what location it

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.570
is originating from.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:23.310
That is used by the rules
engine to define and apply

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:25.440
various policies,
so that it can be

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:29.640
used to protect all the
critical data and applications,

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:33.090
whether it's across your
G Suite apps and services

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:35.940
or they could be your internal
apps that are built on GCP

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:37.980
or using the
Identity-Aware Proxy

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:43.240
or they could be other types
of third-party applications.

00:25:43.240 --> 00:25:48.630
So now, Cloud Identity helps
meet every organization's

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:52.950
unique operational as well
as compliance requirements.

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:56.250
There might be several
scenarios that your organization

00:25:56.250 --> 00:25:57.490
may want to support.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:26:00.030
For example, you may have
scenarios and policies,

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:03.450
which determine that temporary
workers can access your work

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:06.060
email only from the
corporate network.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.910
You may determine that
full-time employees can

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:11.760
work from anywhere,
from any device,

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:13.950
provided these devices
have certain security

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:16.860
policies applied, such
as the disk is encrypted

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:19.230
or screen locks are applied.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:23.340
Employees can access apps only
from company-issued devices.

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:26.700
Or traveling employees can
access app from locations--

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:29.490
cannot access apps
from locations,

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.160
which are deemed to have
a high risk profile.

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.480
And lastly, some employees
cannot access apps outside

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:37.380
of regular business hours.

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:40.110
I'll walk you through a quick
demo of one of these scenarios

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:42.570
here and, for the
rest of the scenarios

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:45.990
here, please visit
our security showcase

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:48.960
at Moscone South, where
a lot of these scenarios

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:51.660
are demonstrated in detail.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:53.580
So now here's the demo.

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:56.310
In the demo, what I'll
be walking you through,

00:26:56.310 --> 00:27:01.370
there is Mary, who is
a full-time employee.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:02.100
Sorry.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:04.030
Mary is a full-time employee.

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:09.840
And she is allowed to
login to her work email

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:11.490
from the corporate network.

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:15.960
However, the corporation or the
organization has set policies

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:20.310
that determine that Mary cannot
access her work email or any

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:23.130
other G Suite services from
locations that are deemed

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:25.920
to have a high risk profile.

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:29.880
So, let me switch
to my demo laptop

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.100
here, where I'll
kind of walk you

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:35.130
through two specific personas.

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:39.100
The first persona is--

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:40.590
OK, perfect.

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:43.960
The first persona is that
of an IT administrator.

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:45.900
And here, the IT
administrator actually

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.560
goes and sets up these policies.

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:50.850
And the second persona
is that of Mary,

00:27:50.850 --> 00:27:52.800
who is now the
user, who is going

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:55.350
to be accessing the different
applications and G Suite

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:58.060
services from her machine.

00:27:58.060 --> 00:28:00.270
So now here, I'm taken
to a screen where,

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:04.740
as an IT administrator, I
will first go and set up

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.400
an access level policy.

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:09.690
And I'll call this
access level policy

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:12.560
to be of name,
Corporate Network.

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:15.090
And I will define this
corporate network policy

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:18.150
to have a condition,
which allows access,

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:21.510
to G Suite services or
maybe any other application,

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:26.260
only from a set of
specified IP subnetworks.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:28.380
So I create the condition.

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:33.480
And I'm specifying a set of IP
subnetworks that are allowed

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:35.160
and that are in the range.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:37.350
Now, once that is
done and I save it,

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:40.860
I can now see a list of all the
different access level policies

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:43.980
that have been configured to
support the different scenarios

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.300
that I had called out earlier.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:49.620
So once this is done, I can go
into the Cloud Identity Admin

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:53.550
console, and, from here, I can
go into the Security section.

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:56.280
In the Security section, I
click on the section which

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:58.530
says Context Aware Access.

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:00.690
Once I click on
Context Aware Access,

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:04.830
I can actually select
the Access Level Policy,

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:08.310
called Corporate Network,
that I had previously defined.

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:11.460
And I apply it to the
root organizational unit,

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:14.280
so that all the users
in my organization

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:18.030
can actually be allowed to
access all the services,

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.100
in this case, the
G Suite services,

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:24.310
only from those
specified IP subnetworks.

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:25.740
And I can take this further.

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:28.500
And I can say that I
only want specific users

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:31.200
or specific organization
units to only access

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:32.010
specific services.

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:36.480
Maybe they can only access Gmail
from those set of IP ranges.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:39.330
So once this is done,
it has been configured.

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:43.080
I will actually now
go and login as Mary

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:45.340
from an incognito window.

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:47.560
So I'm going to be
logging into Gmail.com.

00:29:50.682 --> 00:29:54.730
And I'm going to be logging
into my domain as Mary.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:08.950
And here you go.

00:30:08.950 --> 00:30:12.170
As you can see, Mary's
trying to access her work

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:14.960
email from this untrusted
network at this event

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:15.830
conference.

00:30:15.830 --> 00:30:17.660
And she is denied access.

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:21.290
On the other hand,
I'm, as Mary, trying

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:23.360
to access
news.Google.com, because I

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:25.250
want to see what's going on.

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:28.280
And I can access the website.

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:29.690
So this is the first demo.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:32.570
And as you can see, with
Access Level Policies,

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:34.670
you can have a
lot of flexibility

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:37.880
to meet your organizational
unique requirements

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:40.530
and compliance needs
to do what you want.

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:42.500
And if you want to see
more of these demos,

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:45.860
please visit our security
showcase at Moscone South,

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:48.230
and you can see this.

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:53.550
I'm now going to move
on to the next feature

00:30:53.550 --> 00:31:00.360
that we are going to talk
about, so Kubernetes.

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:02.760
So I think, in this
morning's keynote,

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:07.050
you heard Urs talk a lot
about how enterprises are now

00:31:07.050 --> 00:31:09.150
starting to move
towards adopting

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:11.130
a microservices architecture.

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:12.420
Containers are growing.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:16.390
Over 70% of enterprises are
using Kubernetes and so on.

00:31:16.390 --> 00:31:19.830
So with Cloud Identity,
we are aiming and striving

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:22.740
to provide IT administrators
with a single pane of glass

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:25.180
to manage your users,
devices, and applications.

00:31:25.180 --> 00:31:28.710
And we're also looking towards
extending this paradigm

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:31.620
to support containers
and Kubernetes.

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:34.750
So I'm going to be walking
you through a demo here.

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:38.590
And consider a journey in
which an organization, as part

00:31:38.590 --> 00:31:41.700
of their cloud transformation,
is actually containerizing

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:44.820
their applications and is
using Kubernetes to orchestrate

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:46.710
and deploy these applications.

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:48.570
Now being an enterprise,
they're probably

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:51.870
looking at using a
hybrid environment that

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:55.060
spans multiple clusters
across multiple clouds.

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:58.320
Their developer teams are
building applications.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:01.350
And these applications are being
deployed in shared clusters.

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:03.306
And they're creating
their own namespaces.

00:32:03.306 --> 00:32:04.680
So now, all of a
sudden, you have

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:07.340
islands of namespaces
that have sprung up

00:32:07.340 --> 00:32:10.020
and that are cropping up
across the organization,

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:13.840
with very limited
governance and control.

00:32:13.840 --> 00:32:15.600
So in this particular
demo, that I'm

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:17.460
going to be walking
you through, I'll

00:32:17.460 --> 00:32:19.830
be going over two personas.

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:23.730
The first persona is that of an
IT administrator, who is also

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:26.670
a cluster admin, who
specifies policies that

00:32:26.670 --> 00:32:30.390
govern who can do
what as part of policy

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:31.980
management for these clusters.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:36.510
And the second persona is that
of a cluster user, who is also

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:39.390
a developer, who is
allowed and authorized

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:43.400
to actually run and deploy
applications on these clusters.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:47.690
So now, let me take you
back to my demo machine.

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:54.930
And I will show you
what this looks like.

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:01.010
So let's start here.

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:04.000
So this is the Cloud Identity
Admin console, from which you

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:07.430
have a unified, holistic,
single pane of glass view

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:10.120
for managing all my
people, all my devices,

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:12.130
and all my applications.

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:14.080
I click on the
Kubernetes card, here,

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:17.650
that also now allows me
to manage my Kubernetes

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:20.780
and Kubernetes deployments.

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:24.854
So I'm taken to a view such
as this, where I can now

00:33:24.854 --> 00:33:26.270
see a list of all
my clusters that

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:28.010
are provisioned and running.

00:33:28.010 --> 00:33:31.040
I can get to see a list of all
the different namespaces that

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.340
have been created across
my prod as well as my test

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:36.280
environments.

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:40.690
And I can also see a list of
the different policies that

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:41.950
have been created.

00:33:41.950 --> 00:33:44.170
In particular, there is
a resource quota policy

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:47.650
that has been created and
enforced across my test

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:49.570
and prod namespaces.

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:54.400
In particular, for example,
there is resource quota policy

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:58.480
that has been set up and
defined such that all my test

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:03.370
namespaces cannot use or request
more than 100 core of CPU

00:34:03.370 --> 00:34:05.500
and 20 gigabytes of RAM.

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:07.630
And now I go to my
Roles view, and I

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:10.239
get to see a list of the
different role binding objects

00:34:10.239 --> 00:34:11.590
that have been created.

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:15.250
So here, in particular, there
has been a role binding object

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:17.500
that has been
assigned to the user

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:20.380
Vidster, who is a cluster user.

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:23.020
And she is now
allowed and authorized

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:27.679
to run her jobs on all the
different test namespaces.

00:34:27.679 --> 00:34:30.010
So now, I'm going
to actually take you

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:33.670
through the experience, as
the cluster user Vidster,

00:34:33.670 --> 00:34:36.350
so that she can actually go
and actually run this job.

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:38.230
So let's see what happens here.

00:34:38.230 --> 00:34:40.639
So I'm going to reconnect.

00:34:40.639 --> 00:34:44.690
And first, as the
cluster user Vidster,

00:34:44.690 --> 00:34:50.580
I am going to run
the job by trying

00:34:50.580 --> 00:34:55.270
to create a pod within
the production namespace.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:58.230
So let's see if that works.

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:02.720
So now, I am going to try to
run the job in the production

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:05.390
namespace, prod-next.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:07.520
And you can see that--

00:35:07.520 --> 00:35:09.780
let me try it again.

00:35:09.780 --> 00:35:12.520
And you can see that
it will error-out,

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:16.170
saying that I do not have the
permission or the credentials

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:17.700
to do so.

00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:23.130
Now, I'm going to try to create
a pod in the test namespace,

00:35:23.130 --> 00:35:26.910
but I'm going to request
more resources than what

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:29.580
has been allocated
for me, by the policy

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:33.450
administrator earlier, in the
Cloud Identity Admin console.

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:37.500
So I am going to
now ask for creating

00:35:37.500 --> 00:35:43.740
a pod in the test namespace
but asking for more resources.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.160
So I do that.

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:47.760
And here, you can see
that the job fails.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:49.380
And it does so,
because it clearly

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:52.020
sees that I'm asking for
more resources than what

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:53.910
was allocated to me.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:58.180
And now, I'm going to try
to create a pod in the test

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:00.990
namespace but
asking for resources

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:03.840
that are well within
what was allocated to me.

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:07.020
So I do this, and,
as I can see, I'm

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:12.030
going to actually create
